using System.Globalization;

using Boutquin.Numerics.Solvers;
using FluentAssertions;

namespace Boutquin.Numerics.Tests.Unit.Solvers;

/// <summary>
/// Before the reduced QP accepts its free weights it verifies them against their reduced KKT system,
/// and it may replace range-space weights by a null-space re-solve. At <c>T = decimal</c> that check
/// must neither accept a wrong vector nor refuse a verifiable one: decimal has an absolute resolution
/// of <c>1e-28</c>, so a low-variance row next to a high-variance asset, a weight below that
/// resolution, or covariance entries of order <c>1e-18</c> must all be measured on their own scale.
/// Every problem is in the decimal domain (<c>|x| ≤ 1e14</c>). Expected values are the exact rational
/// KKT optimum (active-set enumeration in <c>fractions.Fraction</c> on the exact inputs), rounded once
/// to decimal; each tolerance is <c>max(1e-12, 4·s)·max|w*|</c>, where <c>s</c> is the largest
/// relative change of the exact optimum under a one-unit change in the last digit of any one input.
/// None comes from the implementation.
/// </summary>
[Trait("Tier", "Unit")]
public sealed class ActiveSetQpDecimalVerificationTests
{
    private static readonly CultureInfo s_invariant = CultureInfo.InvariantCulture;

